.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt FCNTL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fcntl ,
.Nm fcntl64
.Nd control a file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn fcntl "int fd" "int cmd" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fcntl
performs
.Fa cmd
on the open descriptor
.Fa fd .
The optional third argument is an integer or a pointer, as specified
by the command.
Musl implements this function through i386 system call 221,
.Nm fcntl64 ;
it declares no separate
.Fn fcntl64
function.
The kernel takes the descriptor in EBX, the command in ECX, and the
third argument in EDX.
.Pp
The supported commands are:
.Bl -tag -width F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
.It Dv F_DUPFD
Duplicate
.Fa fd
onto the lowest unused descriptor at or above the integer argument.
The new descriptor shares the open-file description and has
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
clear.
Return the new descriptor.
.It Dv F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
As
.Dv F_DUPFD ,
but set
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the new descriptor.
.It Dv F_GETFD
Return
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
if it is set, or 0 otherwise.
.It Dv F_SETFD
Set or clear
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
according to that bit in the integer argument.
Ignore other bits.
.It Dv F_GETFL
Return the access mode,
.Dv O_RDONLY ,
.Dv O_WRONLY ,
or
.Dv O_RDWR ,
combined with the stored
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
flags.
A descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
reports
.Dv O_RDONLY
and
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Dv F_SETFL
Replace the open-file description's
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
flags with those bits from the integer argument.
Ignore other bits, including the access mode and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Dv F_SETLK , F_SETLK64
Set or remove the record lock the pointed-to
.Vt struct flock
describes: a read lock for
.Dv F_RDLCK ,
a write lock for
.Dv F_WRLCK ,
or none for
.Dv F_UNLCK ,
over the bytes it names.
A lock that conflicts with another owner's fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
.It Dv F_SETLKW , F_SETLKW64
As
.Dv F_SETLK ,
but wait for a conflicting lock to go.
A wait that would close a cycle of processes waiting for each other's
locks fails with
.Er EDEADLK ,
when it begins or when the lock it waits for passes to another owner
while it sleeps, as on Linux;
a signal ends the wait with
.Er EINTR ,
which
.Dv SA_RESTART
restarts.
.It Dv F_GETLK , F_GETLK64
Report the first lock of another owner that the described lock would
conflict with: its type, its range from the start of the file, with a
length of 0 for a lock to the end, and its owner's process ID, -1 for
an open file description's.
When none would conflict, set
.Va l_type
to
.Dv F_UNLCK
and leave the other fields as they were.
.It Dv F_OFD_SETLK , F_OFD_SETLKW , F_OFD_GETLK
The same three for open file description locks, which belong to the
open-file description rather than the process.
.Va l_pid
must be 0.
.It Dv F_ADD_SEALS
Add the seals in the integer argument to the tmpfs file
.Fa fd
is open on, which needs
.Fa fd
open for writing, as
.Xr memfd_create 2
describes.
.It Dv F_GET_SEALS
Return the seals of the tmpfs file
.Fa fd
is open on.
.El
.Pp
Every other command is refused with
.Er EINVAL .
A descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
takes
.Dv F_DUPFD ,
.Dv F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC ,
.Dv F_GETFD ,
.Dv F_SETFD
and
.Dv F_GETFL
and returns
.Er EBADF
for every other command, as on Linux.
.Pp
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
belongs to one descriptor and causes it to close during
.Xr execve 2 .
The file offset and status flags belong to the shared open-file
description, so changes are visible through descriptors duplicated by
.Xr dup 2
or inherited by
.Xr fork 2 .
.Dv O_APPEND
selects the file end for regular-file writes.
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
affects operations that implement nonblocking I/O, including socket
connection and receive operations.
.Ss Record locks
A lock is advisory: it stops another lock, never a read or a write.
.Va l_whence
is
.Dv SEEK_SET ,
.Dv SEEK_CUR
or
.Dv SEEK_END ,
and
.Va l_start
is counted from the start of the file, the description's offset or the
file's end.
A positive
.Va l_len
covers that many bytes from
.Va l_start ,
a negative one the bytes before it, and 0 every byte from
.Va l_start
on, however far the file grows.
Two owners' locks conflict when their ranges overlap and one of them is
a write lock.
Setting a read lock needs a descriptor open for reading and a write
lock one open for writing.
.Pp
A POSIX lock belongs to the process that set it.
Another lock of the same process over the same bytes replaces it,
splitting or shortening what it covered, and adjacent locks of one type
are joined.
Every
.Xr close 2
by the process of any descriptor of the file drops all of its locks on
the file, and so do
.Xr dup2 2
over such a descriptor, close-on-exec and exit; the close of a
descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
drops none, as on Linux.
.Xr fork 2
does not pass the locks to the child;
.Xr execve 2
keeps them.
.Pp
An open file description lock belongs to the description: descriptors
made by
.Xr dup 2
and
.Xr fork 2
share it, a second
.Xr open 2
of the file is another owner even in the same process, and the lock goes
with the description's last reference.
Open file description locks and POSIX locks conflict with each other.
.Xr flock 2
locks meet neither.
.Pp
A lock is on the file, found by its mount and the inode number it had
when the description was opened.
On KRFFS, whose inode numbers change when a file is renamed on the
boot root or moved on a mounted volume, a lock taken before such a
change does not meet one taken through a description opened after it.
A descriptor of a pipe, a socket, a device or a terminal is locked by
the object it is open on.
.Pp
.Dv F_GETLK ,
.Dv F_SETLK
and
.Dv F_SETLKW
(5, 6 and 7) take the 16-byte
.Vt struct flock
with 32-bit offsets:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int16_t l_type, l_whence;
int32_t l_start, l_len;
int32_t l_pid;
.Ed
.Pp
The 64-bit forms (12, 13 and 14) and the open file description commands
(36, 37 and 38) take the 24-byte
.Vt struct flock64 ,
whose offsets are 64 bits at byte offsets 4 and 12 and whose
.Va l_pid
is at 20.
Musl's
.Dv F_GETLK ,
.Dv F_SETLK
and
.Dv F_SETLKW
are the 64-bit forms on i386.
The deadlock search follows ten waits, Linux's bound, so a longer cycle
is not found; it is made only for POSIX locks, as on Linux.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The duplication commands return a new descriptor.
.Dv F_GETFD
and
.Dv F_GETFL
return the requested flags.
The other supported commands return 0.
When a set lock command fails, no lock has changed.
On failure,
.Fn fcntl
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno ,
except that musl's
.Dv F_GETOWN
fallback returns the raw -22 of
.Er EINVAL
for an open descriptor without setting it.
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/lockmusl.c
sets record locks from two processes and checks the conflict, the
report of
.Dv F_GETLK ,
a split, the close that drops a process's locks, a wait, one
.Er EDEADLK
for two processes that wait for each other, and open file description
locks against a second open and a POSIX lock.
.Pp
The default suite's
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
sets
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
with
.Dv F_SETFD ,
reads it back with
.Dv F_GETFD ,
then duplicates the descriptor with
.Xr dup2 2
and checks that the new descriptor's flag is clear.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOVERFLOW]"
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A set lock command met a conflicting lock.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH
and the command is not one it takes, or a read lock was asked for on a
descriptor not open for reading or a write lock on one not open for
writing.
.It Bq Er EDEADLK
.Dv F_SETLKW
would wait in a cycle of processes waiting for each other's locks.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A lock command cannot read or write the lock structure.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended a lock wait.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The command is unsupported; a seal command's descriptor is not open on
a tmpfs file, or
.Dv F_ADD_SEALS
was given a bit that is no seal; a duplication command's minimum
descriptor is negative or at or above the process's descriptor limit; a
lock's type or
.Va l_whence
is none of the three, its range begins before byte 0, or
.Dv F_GETLK
asked about
.Dv F_UNLCK ;
or an open file description lock command has a nonzero
.Va l_pid .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
No free descriptor exists at or above the requested minimum and below
the limit, or the required descriptor storage could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOLCK
The kernel heap has no room for a lock.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Dv F_ADD_SEALS
was given a descriptor not open for writing, or the file has
.Dv F_SEAL_SEAL .
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
A lock's range passes the largest offset, or
.Dv F_GETLK
with the 32-bit structure found a lock it cannot describe.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr flock 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2
